#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/features_on_3d_surface.rst:22
msgid "Plotting features on a 3-D surface"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/features_on_3d_surface.rst:24
msgid ""
"In addition to draping a dataset (grid or image) on top of a topographic "
"surface, you may want to add additional features like coastlines, symbols, "
"and text annotations. This tutorial shows how to use :meth:`pygmt.Figure."
"coast`, :meth:`pygmt.Figure.plot3d`, and :meth:`pygmt.Figure.text` to add "
"these features on a 3-D surface created by :meth:`pygmt.Figure.grdview`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/features_on_3d_surface.rst:30
msgid "This tutorial builds a 3-D map with additional features in four steps:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/features_on_3d_surface.rst:32
msgid "Creating a 3-D surface"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/features_on_3d_surface.rst:33
msgid "Adding coastlines on a 3-D surface"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/features_on_3d_surface.rst:34
msgid "Adding symbols on a 3-D surface"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/features_on_3d_surface.rst:35
msgid "Adding text annotations on a 3-D surface"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/features_on_3d_surface.rst:39
msgid ""
"import pandas as pd\n"
"import pygmt\n"
"from pygmt.params import Axis, Frame"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/features_on_3d_surface.rst:50
msgid "1. Creating a 3-D surface"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/features_on_3d_surface.rst:52
msgid ""
"In the first step, we create a 3-D topographic map using :meth:`pygmt.Figure."
"grdview`. We use a region around Taiwan to demonstrate adding features on a "
"3-D surface."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/features_on_3d_surface.rst:57
msgid ""
"# Define the study area in degrees East or North\n"
"region_2d = [119, 123, 21, 26]  # [lon_min, lon_max, lat_min, lat_max]\n"
"\n"
"# Download elevation grid for the study region with a resolution of 5 arc-"
"minutes.\n"
"grd_relief = pygmt.datasets.load_earth_relief(resolution=\"05m\", "
"region=region_2d)\n"
"\n"
"# Determine the 3-D region from the minimum and maximum values of the relief "
"grid\n"
"region_3d = [*region_2d, grd_relief.min().to_numpy(), grd_relief.max()."
"to_numpy()]\n"
"\n"
"fig = pygmt.Figure()\n"
"\n"
"# Set up a colormap for topography and bathymetry that matches the grid "
"range\n"
"pygmt.makecpt(\n"
"    cmap=\"gmt/etopo1\",\n"
"    series=[grd_relief.min().to_numpy(), grd_relief.max().to_numpy()],\n"
")\n"
"\n"
"# Create a 3-D surface\n"
"fig.grdview(\n"
"    projection=\"M12c\",  # Mercator projection with a width of 12 cm\n"
"    region=region_3d,\n"
"    grid=grd_relief,\n"
"    cmap=True,\n"
"    surftype=\"surface\",\n"
"    shading=\"+a0/270+ne0.6\",\n"
"    perspective=[157.5, 30],  # Azimuth and elevation for the 3-D plot\n"
"    zsize=\"1.5c\",\n"
"    facade_fill=\"darkgray\",\n"
"    frame=Frame(axes=\"wSnE\", axis=Axis(annot=True, tick=True)),\n"
")\n"
"\n"
"# Add a colorbar\n"
"fig.colorbar(perspective=True, annot=1000, tick=500, label=\"Elevation\", "
"unit=\"m\")\n"
"\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/features_on_3d_surface.rst:100
msgid "2. Adding coastlines on a 3-D surface"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/features_on_3d_surface.rst:102
msgid ""
"Next, we add coastlines using :meth:`pygmt.Figure.coast` with a matching "
"``perspective`` setting. Here we set the z-level to 0 so coastlines are "
"drawn at sea level."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/features_on_3d_surface.rst:108
msgid ""
"# Add coastlines on top of the 3-D surface\n"
"# Use an explicit perspective to match grdview (azimuth=157.5, "
"elevation=30)\n"
"# and set the z-level to 0 so the coastlines are drawn at sea level.\n"
"fig.coast(perspective=[157.5, 30, 0], resolution=\"high\", shorelines=\"1/1p,"
"black\")\n"
"\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/features_on_3d_surface.rst:122
msgid "3. Adding symbols on a 3-D surface"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/features_on_3d_surface.rst:124
msgid ""
"In the third step, we add star symbols on top of the same 3-D map. To plot "
"symbols on a 3-D surface, use :meth:`pygmt.Figure.plot3d`. The z-coordinate "
"should be set to a value at or above the maximum elevation to ensure the "
"symbols are visible. Note that 3-D rendering in GMT/PyGMT uses a painter's "
"algorithm (depth sorting) rather than true 3-D occlusion. From some "
"viewpoints, symbols that should be hidden behind terrain may still appear "
"visible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/features_on_3d_surface.rst:133
msgid ""
"# Sample point data: five coastal cities around Taiwan\n"
"cities = pd.DataFrame(\n"
"    {\n"
"        \"longitude\": [121.74, 121.61, 121.14, 120.30, 120.53],\n"
"        \"latitude\": [25.13, 23.99, 22.76, 22.63, 24.27],\n"
"        \"name\": [\"Keelung\", \"Hualien\", \"Taitung\", \"Kaohsiung\", "
"\"Taichung Port\"],\n"
"    }\n"
")\n"
"\n"
"# Use one common z-level so all stars are drawn at the same height above the "
"surface.\n"
"cities[\"z\"] = grd_relief.max().to_numpy()\n"
"\n"
"# Add five identical star symbols on top of the 3-D surface\n"
"fig.plot3d(\n"
"    x=cities.longitude,\n"
"    y=cities.latitude,\n"
"    z=cities.z,\n"
"    style=\"a0.65c\",\n"
"    fill=\"gold\",\n"
"    pen=\"0.8p,black\",\n"
"    perspective=True,\n"
")\n"
"\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/features_on_3d_surface.rst:165
msgid "4. Adding text annotations on a 3-D surface"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/features_on_3d_surface.rst:167
msgid ""
"In the final step, we add text labels to the same 3-D map. To add text "
"annotations on a 3-D surface, use :meth:`pygmt.Figure.text` with "
"``perspective=True``. Note that the current implementation of ``text`` does "
"not support a ``z`` parameter for controlling the vertical position of text "
"labels. The text will be placed at the base of the 3-D plot (z=0)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tutorials/advanced/features_on_3d_surface.rst:175
msgid ""
"# Add text labels for cities\n"
"# Note: text is placed at z=0 (base level) since z parameter is not yet "
"supported\n"
"fig.text(\n"
"    x=cities.longitude,\n"
"    y=cities.latitude,\n"
"    text=cities.name,\n"
"    perspective=True,\n"
"    font=\"11p,Times-Bold,red\",\n"
")\n"
"\n"
"fig.show()"
msgstr ""

